                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_imbalanced:    `<strong>That's the CVF reading.</strong> You named the culture as imbalanced for the new mandate, not broken — you are adding Market and Hierarchy to a clan that already earns 98%, not replacing it. Show me which quadrants you are adding, not which culture you are replacing.`,
                    coach_r1_broken:        `<strong>You wrote that the culture is the problem.</strong> The same culture produced 98% satisfaction for eleven years — better than any unit in the company. Is it broken, or simply over-weighted to Clan and Adhocracy for a mandate that now also demands Market and Hierarchy?`,
                    coach_r1_fine:          `<strong>“Don't touch it” misses the mandate.</strong> The 98% is real, but at today's productivity you clear 3,300 cases a month against 10,000 required. Stay purely Clan and the throughput gap is guaranteed and the SLA penalties hit. The culture must add, not freeze.`,
                    coach_r1_market:        `<strong>Wishful thinking.</strong> This unit has never published a metric, met an un-negotiated deadline, or run under an external SLA — it is not already a Market culture. Telling people “we hit numbers now” without building the capability is how you lose both the people and the number.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Imbalanced, not broken — and the two quadrants to ADD are Market and Hierarchy. That is the lens that lets you scale the clan without killing it: add control and results without replacing the relationships that earn the 98%.`,
                    coach_r1_partial:       `<strong>Half the gap.</strong> You're adding one of the two missing quadrants but not the other. Market without Hierarchy gives you ambition with no repeatable process; Hierarchy without Market gives you process with no external results discipline. The mandate needs both.`,
                    coach_r1_clanadhoc:     `<strong>That's doubling down on what already over-weights you.</strong> More Clan and Adhocracy deepens the very imbalance the mandate is straining against. You don't need more relationships and craft — you need to ADD the Market and Hierarchy the clan never built.`,
                    coach_r1_pretend:       `<strong>Pretending the tension away is the most common failure.</strong> If you won't name out loud that the unit is being asked to become partly a Market culture, the resistance goes underground and the change stalls. Naming the contradiction is itself an intervention.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A deliberate balance.</strong> You tilted toward control to scale — process, a dashboard, an external SLA — while protecting the craft lane that keeps the engineers and the 98%. That is the both/and position: how far you can move without breaking the clan.`,
                    coach_r2_overrotate:    `<strong>You moved 75+ of your 100 points to control and results.</strong> Throughput is on track — but your two senior engineers, who hold 30% of your top hospital relationships, just resigned and satisfaction fell. You hit a number and lost the asset that made the number worth having. How far could you have moved without breaking the clan?`,
                    coach_r2_tooaggressive: `<strong>A touch too far, too fast.</strong> A two-thirds tilt to control gets the throughput moving but already strains trust and the satisfaction score. You're close to the both/and zone — ease back a little toward the clan and you keep the engineers as you scale.`,
                    coach_r2_clanlean:      `<strong>A cautious tilt.</strong> Leaning slightly toward the clan keeps trust healthy, but it under-powers the scale-up — the throughput gap closes slowly and the SLA is at risk. You have a little more room to move toward control before you'd break anything.`,
                    coach_r2_stayclan:      `<strong>You stayed in Clan and Adhocracy.</strong> Trust is safe, but the throughput gap is guaranteed and the 24-hour SLA is breaching — USD 25,000 a time. Protecting the culture without adding any control is how you miss the mandate the unit was given.`,
                    coach_r2_callcenter:    `<strong>A scripted call center is exactly “becoming a call center.”</strong> It scales fast on paper, but it is the thing the two key engineers said would make them leave — and it strips the craft that earns the 98%. Add repeatable capacity without industrializing the relationships.`,
                    coach_r2_hireonly:      `<strong>The budget will not fund 120 hires.</strong> At today's productivity the math needs an 86% headcount increase corporate won't pay for — and throwing bodies at an un-redesigned process doesn't scale it. You have to redesign the work, not just staff it.`,
                    coach_r2_nodash:        `<strong>No published dashboard, no external discipline.</strong> Negotiating every deadline case by case is the old clan habit — under a 24-hour SLA it breaches and the penalties stack up. Make the response times visible so the unit can actually manage to them.`,
                    coach_r2_secret:        `<strong>Surprising people with private metrics breaks trust.</strong> Tracking results is right; hiding them and ambushing staff turns a Market move into a fear move. Publish the dashboard openly so it builds ownership, not resentment.`,
                    coach_r2_nolane:        `<strong>No protected craft lane.</strong> Push every complex patient through the standard process and the two senior engineers — who built the 98% on craft — feel the unit has become the call center they feared. Protect a lane for the hard cases.`,
                    coach_r2_cutqa:         `<strong>That is not a service miss — that is a regulatory finding.</strong> Trimming the complaint-handling controls under 21 CFR 820 to go faster downgrades adverse-event reporting. Not all “control” is bureaucracy; some of it is non-negotiable. Keep that one and cut elsewhere.`,
                    coach_r3_spanning:      `<strong>That is behavioral complexity.</strong> Your leaders are over-investing in Producer/Director AND Mentor/Facilitator — enacting competing roles in the same quarter. That's what lets you ship the SLA dashboard and keep the engineers. Effective managers play contradictory roles credibly.`,
                    coach_r3_clanonly:      `<strong>Lovely unit — but nobody is playing Producer or Director.</strong> Every leader chose Mentor and Facilitator, so the SLA dashboard never ships and the CEO stops trusting you. Quinn's point is behavioral complexity: who is enacting the roles that feel unnatural this quarter?`,
                    coach_r3_marketonly:    `<strong>All Producer and Director loses the people.</strong> A leadership team that only drives results and direction breaks the clan that earns the 98% — the engineers walk and the satisfaction collapses. Someone has to be playing Mentor and Facilitator at the same time.`,
                    coach_r3_hieronly:      `<strong>All Monitor and Coordinator gives you process, not movement.</strong> A purely Hierarchy portfolio standardizes everything but neither inspires the people (Clan) nor drives the external results (Market). Behavioral complexity means spanning the quadrants, not picking one.`,
                    coach_r3_promise:       `<strong>Promising the full number on the original date is a trust trap.</strong> When you can't deliver it, the CEO trusts you less than if you'd committed to a credible staged trajectory with leading indicators. Director and Monitor together beat heroics.`,
                    coach_r3_deflect:       `<strong>Asking for 18 more months loses the CEO.</strong> The board's patience is finite and the first 25,000 devices go live in 120 days. Protecting the culture by refusing the mandate isn't both/and — it's resolving the paradox toward Clan, and it forfeits the contract.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>“Anyone who won't scale can leave” just cost you 30% of your relationships.</strong> The two senior engineers hold the top hospital ties and built the 98%. Holding the line on them isn't toughness — it's destroying the asset the number depends on.`,
                    coach_r3_exempt:        `<strong>Exempting them keeps them, but isolated.</strong> Walling the engineers off from all change protects two people while signalling the craft is incompatible with the future — and you lose their power to mentor the new analysts. Enroll them as leaders of the transition instead.`,
                    coach_r3_speed:         `<strong>You downgraded a near-miss to keep triage fast.</strong> Under 21 CFR 820 that is a regulatory event, not a service miss. The faster process you built just mis-logged an adverse-event report. Which control will you deliberately keep when you cut the others?`,
                    coach_r3_freeze:        `<strong>Freezing the whole scale-up is the over-correction.</strong> Keeping the regulatory control is right, but halting everything to re-certify forfeits the throughput you need by Q4. Keep the non-negotiable control and flex the rest — don't stop the transition.`,
                    coach_r4_recalibrate:   `<strong>You recalibrated instead of whipsawing.</strong> Satisfaction slipped to 91% and you made the smallest adjustment that addresses the slip without un-doing the transition. Stability of intent through turbulence is exactly what holds a both/and change together.`,
                    coach_r4_reverse:       `<strong>You reversed every process change at the first dip.</strong> Now you have neither the old culture nor the new capability — you have chaos. Recalibrating the balance is not the same as abandoning it. What was the smallest adjustment that addressed the slip?`,
                    coach_r4_harder:        `<strong>Pushing control harder on a slip finishes breaking the clan.</strong> A 7-point satisfaction dip means you moved too fast, not too slow — leaning even harder into Market/Hierarchy is how you lose the engineers for good. Ease the balance back, don't double down.`,
                    coach_r4_announce:      `<strong>An all-hands announcement is not a culture change.</strong> Three weeks later the pulse survey shows “confident we can grow” still at 18%. Culture is enacted in the roles you play every day, not declared once. What did each leader do differently this week?`,
                    coach_r4_bigbang:       `<strong>Switching the whole unit on day one ignores the context.</strong> A big-bang on a clan that has never run a standardized process breaks trust and floods the SLA. Sequence it — triage and craft lane first, then the dashboard, then full SLA at go-live.`,
                    coach_r4_throughputonly: `<strong>Watching throughput only is how the clan quietly dies.</strong> The number rises while satisfaction and trust slide unwatched until the engineers are already gone. Lead indicators have to include the stakeholder satisfaction that the whole unit was built on.`,
                    coach_r4_satonly:       `<strong>Watching satisfaction only loses the mandate.</strong> Protecting the 98% while ignoring throughput and effectiveness is resolving the paradox toward Clan — the board sees no trajectory to the number and the contract is at risk. Watch all four.`,
                    coach_r4_whipsaw:       `<strong>Low steadiness reads as whipsawing.</strong> Reversing course every time an indicator dips leaves the unit with neither the old culture nor the new capability. Hold the rebalanced intent steady and adjust at the margin, not the core.`,
                    coach_budget_over:      `<strong>You've blown the USD 2.0M transition budget.</strong> Corporate won't top it up — “just hire 120 people” was always going to overrun it — so capacity you planned gets deferred and throughput slips. Sequence the spend; the both/and plan (triage + dashboard + craft lane) fits inside the cap.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Imbalanced-not-broken; named Market + Hierarchy as the gaps.`,
